@anon.43c633cc9d0c07b847282a0596e0d119.1111 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [952 x i8] }> <{ [952 x i8] c"Measures the layouted size of content.\0A\0AThe `measure` function lets you determine the layouted size of content. Note\0Athat an infinite space is assumed, therefore the measured height/width may\0Anot necessarily match the final height/width of the measured content. If you\0Awant to measure in the current layout dimensions, you can combine `measure`\0Aand [`layout`].\0A\0A# Example\0AThe same content can have a different size depending on the [context] that\0Ait is placed into. For example, in the example below `[#content]` is of\0Acourse bigger when we increase the font size.\0A\0A```example\0A#let content = [Hello!]\0A#content\0A#set text(14pt)\0A#content\0A```\0A\0AFor this reason, you can only measure when context is available.\0A\0A```example\0A#let thing(body) = context {\0A  let size = measure(body)\0A  [Width of \22#body\22 is #size.width]\0A}\0A\0A#thing[Hey] \\\0A#thing[Welcome]\0A```\0A\0AThe measure function returns a dictionary with the entries `width` and\0A`height`, both of type [`length`]." }>, align 1
